  shape before I pick which piece to brainstorm first.

  [1mThe fork that decides everything downstream:[0m whether this app has a server and multiple people, or is one person's
  page in one browser. It's not a preference — it determines whether "notify" means a DOM badge you can build in an
  afternoon, or event storage, subscription records, delivery channels, and read-state that survives a reload on another
  device. Going single-user first and adding a backend later means rewriting the task store, the identity layer, and
  the notification store — essentially all of it. Going multi-user when one user was the real need buys you a server, a
  schema, and auth you'll maintain forever.

  - [1mSingle-user, in-browser[0m — cheapest by far; localStorage tasks, in-page notification list/badge. Rules out
    cross-device and any notification that arrives while the tab is closed. Cheap to prototype, expensive to upgrade.
  - [1mMulti-user with a backend[0m — real subscriptions, cross-device, delivery while away. Costs a server, storage, and
    identity before the first notification exists. Hard to change back, but nothing needs redoing later.
  - [1mSingle-user now, storage boundary kept swappable[0m — middle path: build in-browser, but put tasks and notifications
    behind a small interface so a server can slot in. Costs a little indirection up front; shrinks but doesn't eliminate
    the later rewrite.

  My recommendation is the third only if a backend is genuinely likely; otherwise the first. Building multi-user
  infrastructure speculatively for a page that currently renders one heading is the expensive mistake here.
